usb: sunxi-udc: don't busy-wait 5 ms for a short packet

With DMA on an OUT endpoint, a transfer that ends in a short packet
leaves it in the FIFO with RxPktRdy set, since the DMA only takes full
packets. dma_got_short_pkt() tells that apart from a full packet the
DMA is still draining by polling RxPktRdy, in the interrupt handler,
for up to 1000 x 5 us, so every short-ended transfer cost 5 ms with
interrupts off on that CPU.

With a CDC NCM gadget many transfers from the host end short: on the
H700 (RG SP) the host's 16 KB NTBs took about 0.6 ms each when full,
but 204 short-ended ones out of 793 in 1.5 s waited 5.4-5.8 ms each,
about 70% of the time, holding reception to 6.8 MB/s (21.5 MB/s the
other way). The FIFO held 44-164 bytes every time.

The DMA drains a full 512-byte packet in about 20 us, so poll for
200 us instead.

--- a/drivers/usb/sunxi_usb/udc/sunxi_udc.c	2026-11-09 19:26:41.490239210 -0500
+++ b/drivers/usb/sunxi_usb/udc/sunxi_udc.c	2026-11-09 19:26:49.838703591 -0500
@@ -882,15 +882,21 @@
  * If not, we consider a short packet comes in and we will
  * deal with it then.
  *
- * FIXME: The longest time to confirm is up to (5us * 1000).
+ * The DMA drains a full packet in about 20us (512 bytes at high speed),
+ * so 200us is plenty to tell a full packet it is taking from a short
+ * one it never will. This used to wait up to 5ms, in the interrupt
+ * handler, for every transfer ending in a short packet: with CDC NCM
+ * that was most of the time spent receiving (6.8 MB/s on the H700).
  */
+#define SUNXI_UDC_RX_READY_TRIES	40	/* x 5us */
+
 #ifdef SW_UDC_DMA_INNER
 static int sunxi_udc_confirm_rx_ready(void)
 {
 	int i = 0;
 	int rx_ready_flag = 1;
 
-	for (i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
+	for (i = 0; i < SUNXI_UDC_RX_READY_TRIES; i++) {
 		if (!USBC_Dev_IsReadDataReady(g_sunxi_udc_io.usb_bsp_hdle, USBC_EP_TYPE_RX)) {
 			rx_ready_flag = 0;
 			break;
